Business Problem
Airlines collect information about passengers' demographics, travel characteristics, service experiences, and flight delays. However, understanding which factors are most strongly associated with passenger satisfaction can help airlines prioritize improvements and provide a better customer experience.

Business Objective
The objective of this project is to identify the factors associated with passenger satisfaction and develop insights that can help an airline improve its services and passenger experience.

Business Questions
The analysis will address the following questions:

What is the overall level of passenger satisfaction?
Does passenger satisfaction differ by gender?
Does passenger satisfaction differ by customer type?
Does passenger satisfaction differ by type of travel?
Does passenger satisfaction differ across travel classes?
Which airline services are most strongly associated with passenger satisfaction?
Are flight delays associated with passenger satisfaction?
Can passenger satisfaction be predicted using passenger, travel, service, and delay information?
Dataset
The dataset contains 25,976 passenger records and 25 variables.

The variables include:

Passenger demographics
Customer type
Type of travel
Travel class
Flight distance
Airline service ratings
Departure delays
Arrival delays
Overall passenger satisfaction
Target Variable
The target variable is:

satisfaction

The target contains two categories:

satisfied
neutral or dissatisfied
This makes the machine learning task a binary classification problem.

Data Understanding
Initial inspection of the dataset revealed:

25,976 observations
25 variables
19 integer variables
1 floating-point variable
5 categorical variables
No duplicate records were identified.
Arrival Delay in Minutes is the only variable containing missing values, with 83 missing observations (approximately 0.32%).
Two identifier-like columns, Unnamed: 0 and id, were also identified and will be assessed during the data preparation stage to determine whether they should be excluded from modeling.

Initial Findings
2.2.1 Business Question: What is the overall level of passenger satisfaction?
56.1% of passengers are neutral or dissatisfied.
43.9% of passengers are satisfied.
The target variable is not perfectly balanced, but both categories have substantial representation.

Passenger Characteristics
The dataset contains:

13,172 female and 12,804 male passengers.
21,177 loyal and 4,799 disloyal customers.
18,038 business and 7,938 personal travellers.
12,495 Business, 11,564 Eco, and 1,917 Eco Plus passengers.
Flight Delays
Flight delays are highly right-skewed.

Both departure and arrival delays have a median of 0 minutes, while the maximum delay exceeds 1,100 minutes. This suggests that most passengers experienced little or no delay, while a smaller number experienced very long delays.

2.2.2 Business Question: Does passenger satisfaction differ by gender?
Passenger satisfaction was compared between male and female passengers.

Gender	Neutral or Dissatisfied	Satisfied
Female	56.46%	43.54%
Male	55.73%	44.27%
The difference in the percentage of satisfied passengers is approximately 0.73 percentage points.

This suggests that gender is not a major differentiating factor in passenger satisfaction in this dataset.

2.2.3 Business Question: How do satisfaction levels differ across customer types?
The analysis compared passenger satisfaction between loyal and disloyal customers.

Key Findings
Loyal Customers: 48.14% were satisfied, while 51.86% were neutral or dissatisfied.
Disloyal Customers: 25.17% were satisfied, while 74.83% were neutral or dissatisfied.
The difference in satisfaction was approximately 22.97 percentage points.
Business Interpretation
Customer type shows a noticeable association with passenger satisfaction. Disloyal customers had substantially lower satisfaction levels than loyal customers.

The airline should investigate the experiences of disloyal customers to identify areas that may require improvement.

2.2.4 Business Question: Does Satisfaction Differ by Type of Travel?
The analysis compared passenger satisfaction between business and personal travellers.

Key Findings
Business Travellers: 58.82% were satisfied.
Personal Travellers: 9.99% were satisfied.
The difference in satisfaction was approximately 48.83 percentage points.
Business Interpretation
Type of travel shows a strong association with passenger satisfaction.

Business travellers had considerably higher satisfaction levels, while personal travellers had much lower satisfaction levels.

The airline should investigate whether differences in service expectations, convenience, pricing, or overall travel experience may explain this pattern.

2.2.5 Business Question: Does Satisfaction Differ Across Travel Classes?
The analysis compared passenger satisfaction across Business, Eco, and Eco Plus classes.

Key Findings
Business: 69.52% were satisfied.
Eco: 19.39% were satisfied.
Eco Plus: 24.78% were satisfied.
Business class had the highest satisfaction level.
Eco class had the lowest satisfaction level.
The difference between Business and Eco satisfaction was approximately 50.13 percentage points.
Business Interpretation
Travel class shows a strong association with passenger satisfaction.

Business-class passengers were considerably more satisfied than passengers travelling in Eco and Eco Plus.

The airline should investigate the passenger experience in Eco and Eco Plus classes to identify areas where improvements could increase satisfaction.

2.2.6 Business Question: Which Individual Airline Services Are Most Strongly Associated with Passenger Satisfaction?
The analysis compared the average ratings of different airline services between satisfied and neutral or dissatisfied passengers.

Key Findings
The services with the largest differences in average ratings were:

Online boarding — 1.35
Inflight entertainment — 1.08
Seat comfort — 0.92
Cleanliness — 0.84
On-board service — 0.83
Leg room service — 0.82
Inflight wifi service — 0.75
Online boarding had the largest difference in average rating between satisfied and neutral or dissatisfied passengers.

Business Interpretation
Several airline services show strong associations with passenger satisfaction.

In particular, online boarding, inflight entertainment, and seat comfort may be important areas for further investigation.

These results show associations rather than causation. Further statistical analysis and machine-learning models will help determine whether these variables provide useful predictive information.

2.2.7 Business Question: Are Flight Delays Associated with Passenger Satisfaction?
The analysis compared departure and arrival delays between satisfied and neutral or dissatisfied passengers.

Key Findings
Neutral or dissatisfied passengers had an average departure delay of 16.02 minutes.
Satisfied passengers had an average departure delay of 12.12 minutes.
Neutral or dissatisfied passengers had an average arrival delay of 16.77 minutes.
Satisfied passengers had an average arrival delay of 12.15 minutes.
The median departure and arrival delay was 0 minutes for both satisfaction groups.
Business Interpretation
Neutral or dissatisfied passengers experienced higher average departure and arrival delays than satisfied passengers.

However, the median delay was zero for both groups, suggesting that many passengers experienced no delay while a smaller number of longer delays increased the averages.

This suggests that severe or unusually long delays may be worth investigating further.

2.2.8 Business Question: Can Passenger Satisfaction Be Predicted Using Passenger, Travel, Service, and Delay Information?
The final business question asks whether passenger satisfaction can be predicted using the available passenger, travel, service, and delay information.

Key Findings
Passenger satisfaction will be treated as a binary classification problem with two outcomes:

Satisfied
Neutral or dissatisfied
Potential predictors include:

Passenger information
Customer type
Type of travel
Travel class
Flight distance
Airline service ratings
Departure delays
Arrival delays
Several machine-learning classification models will be considered, including:

Logistic Regression
Decision Tree
Random Forest
The models will be evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and confusion matrices.

Business Interpretation
The purpose of the prediction model is to determine whether the available passenger, travel, service, and delay information can accurately predict passenger satisfaction.

If the model performs well, the airline may be able to identify patterns associated with dissatisfaction and use these insights to prioritize areas for further investigation and service improvement.

The model will identify predictive relationships and should not be interpreted as proof that any individual factor causes passenger satisfaction.

3. Data Preparation
Data preparation is the process of getting the dataset ready for analysis and machine learning.

In this stage, we will:

Remove unnecessary columns.
Check and handle missing values.
Check categorical variables.
Prepare the target variable.
Separate the features from the target.
Split the data into training and testing sets.
Prepare the data for machine-learning models.
The goal is to make sure the data is clean, consistent, and in a suitable format for modeling.

3.1 Remove Unnecessary Columns
The Unnamed: 0 and id columns were removed because they are identifiers and do not provide meaningful information about passenger satisfaction.

3.2 Check Missing Values
The dataset contained 83 missing values in Arrival Delay in Minutes, representing approximately 0.32% of the 25,976 passenger records.

3.3 Handling Missing Values
The missing Arrival Delay in Minutes values were replaced using the median.

The median was selected because the delay data is right-skewed and contains some extreme delay values. The median is less affected by extreme values than the mean.

3.4 Check Categorical Variables
The categorical variables were checked for their unique values.

The categorical features were:

Gender
Customer Type
Type of Travel
Class
The categories were consistent and suitable for encoding.

3.5 Prepare Target Variable
The target variable, satisfaction, was converted from text into numerical values:

1 = Satisfied
0 = Neutral or dissatisfied
This allows the problem to be treated as a binary classification task.

3.6 Separate Features and Target
The dataset was divided into:

X — predictor variables
y — target variable
The features contained 25,976 passenger records and 22 predictor variables.

3.7 Split Data into Training and Testing Sets
The data was divided into:

80% training data
20% testing data
This resulted in:

Training data: 20,780 records
Testing data: 5,196 records
A random_state of 42 was used to make the split reproducible.

Stratification was also used to maintain a similar satisfaction distribution in both datasets.

3.8 Check Satisfaction Distribution After Split
The satisfaction distribution remained almost identical between the training and testing datasets.

Training: 56.10% neutral/dissatisfied and 43.90% satisfied
Testing: 56.10% neutral/dissatisfied and 43.90% satisfied
This confirms that stratification worked successfully.

3.9 Encode Categorical Variables
One-Hot Encoding was used to convert categorical variables into numerical features.

The 4 categorical features were transformed while the 18 numerical features were retained.

After encoding, the dataset contained 23 processed features.

3.10 Apply the Preprocessing
A ColumnTransformer was used to apply different preprocessing steps to numerical and categorical variables.

Numerical variables were standardized using StandardScaler.
Categorical variables were converted using OneHotEncoder.
The preprocessing was incorporated into a machine-learning pipeline.

The processed datasets contained:

Training: 20,780 records × 23 features
Testing: 5,196 records × 23 features
4. Predictive Modeling
The predictive modeling stage investigates whether passenger satisfaction can be predicted using passenger, travel, service, and delay information.

The target is a binary classification problem:

1 = Satisfied
0 = Neutral or dissatisfied
4.1 Logistic Regression
Logistic Regression was selected as the first classification model because it is suitable for predicting an outcome with two categories.

The numerical features were scaled before training because the variables have very different ranges.

The model was trained using the training dataset and evaluated using the unseen testing dataset.

4.2 Model Predictions
The trained Logistic Regression model was used to predict satisfaction outcomes for the testing dataset.

The predictions were stored in y_pred.

4.3 Logistic Regression Performance
The Logistic Regression model achieved:

Metric	Score
Accuracy	87.24%
Precision	86.18%
Recall	84.48%
F1-score	85.32%
Key Finding
The model achieved an overall accuracy of 87.24%, indicating that it correctly classified approximately 87 out of every 100 passengers in the testing dataset.

The precision of 86.18% indicates that most passengers predicted as satisfied were actually satisfied.

The recall of 84.48% indicates that the model identified most of the passengers who were actually satisfied.

The F1-score of 85.32% indicates a good balance between precision and recall.

Business Interpretation
The results suggest that passenger characteristics, travel information, service ratings, and flight delays contain useful information for predicting passenger satisfaction.

However, the model is not perfect and makes some incorrect predictions. Further evaluation using a confusion matrix and comparison with other classification models will help determine whether the predictive performance can be improved.

Important: Predictive performance shows that variables are useful for prediction. It does not prove that these variables directly cause passenger satisfaction or dissatisfaction.

4.10 Confusion Matrix for the Final Model
The Decision Tree was selected as the final model because it performed better than Logistic Regression across all evaluation metrics.

The confusion matrix was used to understand how well the Decision Tree classified passengers into the two satisfaction categories.

The resulting confusion matrix was:

Predicted Neutral/Dissatisfied	Predicted Satisfied
Actual Neutral/Dissatisfied	2,792	123
Actual Satisfied	194	2,087
The results can be interpreted as follows:

True Negatives: 2,792 — neutral or dissatisfied passengers correctly identified.
False Positives: 123 — passengers predicted as satisfied but actually neutral or dissatisfied.
False Negatives: 194 — passengers predicted as neutral or dissatisfied but actually satisfied.
True Positives: 2,087 — satisfied passengers correctly identified.
Key Finding
The Decision Tree correctly classified a large majority of passengers in both satisfaction categories while making relatively few incorrect predictions.

This supports the model's strong testing performance of 93.90% accuracy.

Business Interpretation
The confusion matrix suggests that the Decision Tree can effectively distinguish between satisfied and neutral or dissatisfied passengers.

The model could therefore be used as a decision-support tool to identify satisfaction patterns and help the airline investigate passengers or service areas that may require attention.

However, the model should not be treated as a perfect predictor of individual passenger satisfaction. Its predictions should be considered alongside business knowledge and other passenger information.

4.11 Model Comparison
Two classification models were evaluated:

Logistic Regression
Decision Tree
Both models were evaluated using the same testing dataset and the same performance metrics.

Model	Accuracy	Precision	Recall	F1-score
Logistic Regression	87.24%	86.18%	84.48%	85.32%
Decision Tree	93.90%	94.43%	91.49%	92.94%
Key Finding
The Decision Tree outperformed Logistic Regression across all four evaluation metrics.

Compared with Logistic Regression, the Decision Tree achieved:

6.66 percentage points higher accuracy
8.25 percentage points higher precision
7.01 percentage points higher recall
7.62 percentage points higher F1-score
Business Interpretation
The Decision Tree provides stronger predictive performance than Logistic Regression for this dataset.

This suggests that the relationships between passenger characteristics, travel information, service ratings, and satisfaction may be better captured by a Decision Tree than by the simpler Logistic Regression model.

4.12 Checking for Overfitting
A model can perform very well on training data but perform poorly on new data. This is known as overfitting.

To check for overfitting, the Decision Tree's training and testing accuracy were compared.

Training Accuracy: 95.34%
Testing Accuracy: 93.90%
Difference: approximately 1.44 percentage points
Key Finding
The difference between training and testing accuracy is relatively small.

This suggests that the Decision Tree is generalizing well to unseen passenger data and does not show strong evidence of overfitting.

Business Interpretation
The Decision Tree is not simply memorizing the passengers in the training dataset.

Its strong performance on the unseen testing dataset suggests that it has learned patterns that can also be applied to new passenger records.

This increases confidence in using the model as a decision-support tool.

4.13 Feature Importance
The Decision Tree's feature importance scores were examined to identify which variables were most useful for making predictions.

The most important features were:

Rank	Feature	Importance
1	Online boarding	40.80%
2	Inflight wifi service	19.89%
3	Type of Travel: Business travel	17.03%
4	Inflight entertainment	4.69%
5	Checkin service	2.97%
6	Customer Type: disloyal Customer	2.18%
7	Flight Distance	2.16%
8	Cleanliness	1.52%
9	Baggage handling	1.22%
10	Leg room service	1.19%
The three most important features — Online boarding, Inflight wifi service, and Business travel — account for approximately 77.6% of the model's total feature importance.

Key Finding
Online boarding was the most important feature in the Decision Tree, with an importance score of approximately 40.80%.

Inflight Wi-Fi service was the second most important feature at approximately 19.89%.

Type of Travel: Business travel was the third most important feature at approximately 17.03%.

Business Interpretation
The model suggests that digital and onboard service experiences are particularly important for predicting passenger satisfaction.

The high importance of online boarding suggests that the airline should pay close attention to the quality and ease of its digital boarding experience.

The importance of inflight Wi-Fi also suggests that connectivity may be an important part of the passenger experience.

The strong importance of business travel is consistent with the earlier EDA, which showed substantial differences in satisfaction between business and personal travelers.

Feature importance represents the variables used by the model for prediction. It does not prove that these variables directly cause passenger satisfaction.

4.14 Final Predictive Modeling Summary
The predictive modeling stage tested whether passenger satisfaction could be predicted using passenger, travel, service, and delay information.

Two classification models were evaluated:

Logistic Regression
Decision Tree
The Decision Tree was selected as the final model because it achieved the strongest performance.

Final Model Performance
Accuracy: 93.90%
Precision: 94.43%
Recall: 91.49%
F1-score: 92.94%
The model also showed only a 1.44 percentage-point difference between training and testing accuracy, suggesting limited overfitting.

Final Model Conclusion
The Decision Tree provides strong predictive performance for passenger satisfaction in this dataset.

The model suggests that online boarding, inflight Wi-Fi service, and type of travel are particularly useful for predicting satisfaction.

These findings can be combined with the earlier exploratory analysis to develop practical recommendations for the airline.

